From: GLM-EXL3-2x-GB10 testing line <tensorfold-zig-testing@local>
Subject: glm-cuda: packed prompt matmuls in two stages, two blocks an SM (Mia 1104 0009, adapted to v1.2.3)

PROVENANCE: ported from MiaAI-Lab/GLM-5.3-Flash-DGX-Sparks-1104,
patches/engine/0009-glm-cuda-packed-prompt-matmuls-in-two-stages-two.patch
(Apache-2.0, (c) MiaAI Lab) against TensorFold v1.2.3 (6ec5fa4cb), 2026-11-06,
by this line. APPLIES CLEAN - no re-anchoring needed (the file set
glm_mm.cu/cuda_mm.zig is untouched by our 4-patch stack; the hunk contexts
matched v1.2.3 verbatim). MECHANISM: glm_mm{4,6,8}_128s2 - the packed-prompt
matmul kernel with __launch_bounds__(256, 2) and 41 KB of shared memory in
two cp.async stages (the 128-row kernels take 61.8 KB in three stages, one
block an SM; a 768-row prompt piece's 384 blocks fit one wave instead of
2.04). Each output's K groups run in the same order, so the sums are
glm_mm{B}_128's bit for bit - Mia measured 0 differing outputs on all eight
shapes. Mia's measurement (RTX PRO 6000, sm_120, 400 W sustained): 1.07-1.09x
on GLM's prompt shapes; under their power cap most of the occupancy gain
became power - the GB10 envelope differs, so the A/B on our shape decides.
STAGING: applies on v1.2.3 + the 4-patch stack (disjoint files; the patch
list order does not matter for this one). OWNER: this line; STATUS:
EXPERIMENTAL, judged by the mms2 window (the content gate + the pp rows,
the definitive protocol).

From 00758acbe88789d68319e5a5e57a409df109b3e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MiaAI-Lab <mia.ailab1@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 14:35:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 09/13] glm cuda: packed prompt matmuls in two stages, two
 blocks an SM (optional)

glm_mm{4,6,8}_128 take 61.8 KB of shared memory in three cp.async stages, one block an SM,
so a 768-row prompt piece's 384 blocks ran 2.04 waves on 188 SMs. glm_mm{4,6,8}_128s2 are
the same kernel in two stages (41.2 KB, 122 registers on sm_120): two blocks an SM. Each
output's K groups run in the same order, so the sums are the same bits; packed prompt
launches (the 128-row bucket) use them.

Measured on an RTX PRO 6000 (sm_120, 400 W, sustained): 1.07-1.09x on GLM's prompt shapes,
0 differing outputs on all eight. Under the power cap most of the occupancy gain becomes
power, so this is optional; contract records for the three entries are still to add.

